# MDC Rule: WordPress/PHP Coding Standards

- Follow the official WordPress PHP coding standards: https://developer.wordpress.org/coding-standards/wordpress-coding-standards/php/
- Where WordPress standards conflict with PSR-12, WordPress standards take precedence. Use PSR-12 only where not in conflict.
- Indent code using 4 spaces, not tabs.
- Use lowercase for all PHP keywords, functions, and constructs.
- Use Yoda conditions only where assignment in conditionals is possible; otherwise, follow current WordPress core standards.
- Always use full PHP opening tags `<?php` (never short tags).
- End files with a single blank line.
- Omit the closing `?>` tag in PHP-only files.
- Use PHP namespaces for all custom classes and functions in plugins/themes.
- Use Composer for autoloading classes where possible.

## Examples

```php
// Namespaces and Composer autoloading
namespace MyPlugin\Service;
class MyService {
    public function do_something() {
        // ...
    }
}
```

```json
// composer.json
{
  "autoload": {
    "psr-4": {"MyPlugin\\": "src/"}
  }
}
// After editing composer.json, run: composer dump-autoload
```

```php
// Yoda condition (assignment in conditional)
if ( true === $is_enabled ) {
    // ...
}
```

```php
// Omit closing PHP tag in PHP-only files
// <?php
// ... PHP code ...
// (no closing ?> tag)
```

This rule applies to all `*.php` files in all subdirectories.







